  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure will bring lots of dry weather, \ncoastal breezes, and near to below normal temperatures through mid \nnext week.\n\nPersistent high pressure will dominate the weather for the next week \nleading to dry conditions and generally near to below normal \ntemperatures. A few showers could brush the coast though starting \nthis weekend as low-level moisture gets pulled in from the Atlantic, \nespecially around Cape Fear. \n\nLow pressure developing near The Bahamas will lead to a tightening \nof the pressure gradient between it and high pressure building from \nthe north. As a result, northeasterly winds will pick up this \nweekend, especially near the coast where gusts to around 35 mph are \nexpected. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minor coastal flooding and hazardous marine/surf \nconditions are expected through at least early next week. \n\nAn extended period of enhanced northeast winds due to strong high \npressure to the north and low pressure developing near The Bahamas \nwill lead to hazardous marine/surf conditions. Also, increasing \nastronomical tides and NE winds will lead to minor coastal flooding.\n\nCoastal Flooding: Minor coastal flooding is expected to continue \nalong the lower Cape Fear River during each high tide cycle, \ninitially along the SE NC coast and lower Cape Fear River and then \nspreading southward along the NE SC coast.  \n\nHigh Surf: Elevated seas reaching to around 10 ft within 20 nm of \nshore could lead to 6+ ft breaking waves on the beaches along with \nsome beach erosion, especially the E-facing beaches in New Hanover \nand Georgetown Counties. \n\nMarine: See the Marine section below for details regarding marine \nhazards.",
